a("Wairarapa", 1012, "2018-02-26", "North Island", None, ["red", "white"], "red", "Pinot Noir, Sauvignon Blanc, Chardonnay, Pinot Gris, Syrah",
  "The Wairarapa lies an hour north-east of Wellington across the Remutaka Range and holds about 1,190 ha of vines, mostly Pinot Noir and Sauvignon Blanc. It contains two registered sub-GIs, Martinborough and Gladstone, and the area around Masterton (Opaki). Boutique producers dominate; the region makes only about 3 percent of New Zealand's wine but a much larger share of its fine Pinot.",
  "A rain shadow behind the Remutaka and Tararua ranges, with strong north-westerly winds that cut yields and keep disease low. Free-draining river-terrace soils over gravel.", ["Ata Rangi", "Dry River", "Escarpment", "Palliser", "Craggy Range (Te Muna)", "Kusuda"], "", ["Martinborough", "Gladstone"], ha=1189)
a("Martinborough", 1011, "2018-02-26", "North Island", "Wairarapa", ["red", "white"], "red", "Pinot Noir, Sauvignon Blanc, Pinot Gris, Chardonnay, Syrah",
  "A small village laid out in the shape of a Union Jack in the 1870s, now one of New Zealand's most important Pinot Noir districts. In 1979 a report by soil scientist Dr Derek Milne compared its climate and free-draining terrace soils to Burgundy; Neil McCallum (Dry River) and Clive Paton (Ata Rangi) planted that year and the next, followed by Martinborough Vineyard and Palliser. Their Pinots, savoury, firm and long-lived, set the standard for New Zealand red in the 1990s. Ata Rangi's Pinot Noir, from the 'Abel' clone said to have been smuggled in from Burgundy, is the region's icon.",
  "The Martinborough Terrace, an old river terrace of silt over deep, free-draining gravel; dry, windy and cool, with frost risk. The Te Muna Road area to the south-east has similar soils.",
  ["Ata Rangi", "Dry River", "Martinborough Vineyard", "Escarpment", "Palliser", "Kusuda", "Schubert", "Te Kairanga"],
  "The 'Abel' or 'Gumboot' clone of Pinot Noir is said to have been confiscated from a traveller's gumboot at Auckland airport by customs officer Malcolm Abel, who propagated it.", pt=(-41.2183, 175.4595))
a("Gladstone", 1014, "2019-01-03", "North Island", "Wairarapa", ["white", "red"], "white", "Sauvignon Blanc, Pinot Noir, Pinot Gris, Riesling",
  "A tiny rural district in the Ruamahanga River valley between Martinborough and Masterton, registered in 2019. A handful of family vineyards make Sauvignon Blanc, Pinot Gris and Pinot Noir on gravelly river terraces.",
  "Alluvial gravels and silts on terraces of the Ruamahanga River; slightly cooler and wetter than Martinborough.", ["Gladstone Vineyard", "Urlar"], "Urlar is one of the few Wairarapa estates with organic and biodynamic certification.", pt=(-41.0773, 175.6582))
a("Nelson", 1006, "2018-06-26", "South Island", None, ["white", "red"], "white", "Sauvignon Blanc, Pinot Noir, Chardonnay, Pinot Gris, Riesling",
  "Nelson, at the top of the South Island, is one of the sunniest places in New Zealand and a small, artistic wine region of about 970 ha. It has two distinct areas: the Waimea Plains, with stony alluvial soils that give fresh Sauvignon and aromatic whites, and the Moutere Hills, where clay-bound gravels give richer Chardonnay and Pinot Noir. German settlers planted vines in the 1840s; the modern era began with Hermann Seifried (1973) and Tim and Judy Finn at Neudorf (1978), whose Moutere Chardonnay is one of the country's best.",
  "Sheltered by mountains on three sides; high sunshine and moderate rain. Moutere gravel-clays (weathered, iron-rich) contrast with the stony Waimea Plains.", ["Neudorf", "Seifried", "Kina Beach", "Greenhough", "Rimu Grove"],
  "Neudorf's Moutere Chardonnay comes from vines on the clay-gravel 'Moutere Gravels', which hold water and give a dense, mineral wine.", ha=970)
a("Marlborough", 1004, "2018-02-26", "South Island", None, ["white", "red", "sparkling", "rose"], "white", "Sauvignon Blanc (about 80 percent), Pinot Noir, Pinot Gris, Chardonnay, Riesling",
  "Marlborough, at the north-eastern tip of the South Island, has about 32,000 ha of vines, 73 percent of New Zealand's vineyard, and more than 1,100 vineyards. Sheep grazed its flat Wairau Valley until 1973, when Montana planted vines at Brancott against advice that the region was too cold and dry. Its first Sauvignon Blanc, from the 1979 vintage, showed something new: intensely aromatic wine, with passion fruit, gooseberry and fresh-cut grass, and searing acidity. Hunter's success in London in 1986 and Cloudy Bay's launch in 1985 brought world attention, and Marlborough Sauvignon became one of the most successful wine styles of the modern era.\n\nThe region has three main sub-areas. The Wairau Valley, along the Wairau River, has stony, free-draining alluvial soils on its northern side (Rapaura) that give the classic tropical style. The Southern Valleys (Brancott, Omaka, Fairhall, Ben Morven, Waihopai), on clay-rich soils, give more textured Sauvignon, Pinot Noir and Chardonnay. The Awatere Valley to the south-east, higher, windier and cooler, gives a leaner, more herbaceous and saline style. Producers such as Dog Point, Greywacke, Clos Henri, Te Whare Ra, Seresin and Churton show that Marlborough can also make complex, barrel-fermented and wild-yeast Sauvignon, serious Pinot Noir and traditional-method sparkling. The flip side is heavy reliance on one grape: the region produced a record crop in 2025 and growers face pressure to diversify.",
  "Very sunny (about 2,400 hours a year), dry (about 650 mm of rain) and with big swings between warm days and cool nights. Soils are young alluvial gravels, silts and clays laid down by the Wairau, Awatere and their tributaries.",
  ["Cloudy Bay", "Brancott Estate", "Dog Point", "Greywacke", "Clos Henri", "Seresin", "Te Whare Ra", "Hunter's", "Fromm", "Churton", "Yealands", "Saint Clair", "Villa Maria", "Astrolabe", "Nautilus"],
  "Ivan Sutherland and James Healy, who left Cloudy Bay to found Dog Point, make Section 94, a barrel-fermented Sauvignon Blanc named after a land-survey section.",
  ["Wairau Valley (informal)", "Southern Valleys (informal)", "Awatere Valley (informal)"], ha=32042)
a("Canterbury", 1017, "2018-02-26", "South Island", None, ["white", "red", "sweet"], "white", "Pinot Noir, Sauvignon Blanc, Riesling, Pinot Gris, Chardonnay",
  "The Canterbury GI covers the broad province around Christchurch, from the Canterbury Plains to Banks Peninsula. French settlers planted vines at Akaroa in 1840, and Lincoln University's research in the 1970s showed the plains could ripen cool-climate grapes. Today almost all quality vineyards are in North Canterbury, a separate GI inside it.",
  "Cool, dry and windy, with the nor'wester (a hot, dry foehn wind) a defining feature.", ["Pegasus Bay", "Giesen", "St Helena (historic)"], "", ["North Canterbury"])
a("North Canterbury", 1016, "2020-06-29", "South Island", "Canterbury", ["white", "red", "sweet"], "red", "Pinot Noir, Riesling, Sauvignon Blanc, Chardonnay, Pinot Gris",
  "North Canterbury, registered in 2020, covers the hills north of Christchurch, including the Waipara Valley and the limestone country around Waikari. With about 1,540 ha, it is a source of some of New Zealand's most intellectually interesting wines: Pyramid Valley's and Bell Hill's limestone-grown Pinot Noir and Chardonnay, and Pegasus Bay's Riesling and Pinot.",
  "Warm, dry summers and long autumns protected from easterly sea breezes by the Teviotdale Hills; limestone, clay and gravels.", ["Pegasus Bay", "Pyramid Valley", "Bell Hill", "Black Estate", "Greystone", "Muddy Water", "Mountford"],
  "The limestone at Waikari and Weka Pass is rare in New Zealand, which is one reason Burgundy-obsessed growers chose it.", ["Waipara Valley"], ha=1539)
a("Waipara Valley", 1010, "2018-12-03", "South Island", "North Canterbury", ["white", "red", "sweet"], "white", "Riesling, Pinot Noir, Sauvignon Blanc, Pinot Gris, Chardonnay",
  "A small, warm valley about an hour north of Christchurch, registered as a GI in 2018 and sitting within North Canterbury. Pegasus Bay, founded by the Donaldson family in 1985, made its name for Riesling (in dry to botrytised styles) and Pinot Noir; Black Estate, Greystone and Mountford followed. The valley is sheltered from cool easterlies by the Teviotdale Hills, so it ripens grapes well for its latitude.",
  "Glacial gravels and clay loams on the valley floor, with limestone-rich clays on the slopes; hot dry summers and the nor'wester wind.", ["Pegasus Bay", "Black Estate", "Greystone", "Mountford", "Muddy Water"], "", match=["Waipara Valley / Waipara", "Waipara"], pt=(-43.06, 172.75))
a("Waitaki Valley North Otago", 1019, "2018-12-03", "South Island", None, ["white", "red"], "red", "Pinot Noir, Pinot Gris, Riesling, Chardonnay",
  "One of New Zealand's newest and most marginal regions, in the Waitaki River valley around Kurow and Duntroon, registered in 2018. Its first vines went in around 2001. Limestone outcrops and a cold, frost-prone climate attracted growers such as Jim Jerram (Ostler) and Michelle Richardson. With fewer than 50 ha, it makes taut Pinot Noir, Pinot Gris and Riesling.",
  "Limestone and schist gravels with windblown loess; very cool, with a short season and frost risk.", ["Ostler", "Valli (Waitaki vineyard)", "Pasquale"], "", match=["Waitaki Valley North Otago / Waitaki Valley", "Waitaki Valley"], pt=(-44.75, 170.55), ha=49)
a("Central Otago", 1007, "2018-10-29", "South Island", None, ["red", "white", "sparkling", "rose"], "red", "Pinot Noir (about 70 to 80 percent), Pinot Gris, Chardonnay, Riesling, Sauvignon Blanc",
  "Central Otago is the world's most southerly commercial wine region, at around 45 degrees south, and its Pinot Noir is New Zealand's most famous red. It is a dramatic place: dry brown hills of schist, rivers and lakes, gold-rush ruins and snow-capped mountains. Jean Désiré Feraud planted vines at Clyde in 1864, but the modern industry began in the 1970s and 1980s with Rolfe and Lois Mills at Rippon on Lake Wanaka (1975) and Alan Brady at Gibbston Valley (first commercial wine in 1987). It now has about 2,100 ha and more than 230 vineyards.\n\nThe climate is semi-continental: hot, dry summer days, cold nights and frost in spring and autumn, so vineyards occupy slopes and terraces that drain cold air. Pinot Noir here is vivid and dark-fruited, with spice and fine tannin. Sub-regions differ. Gibbston, in the Kawarau Gorge near Queenstown, is the coolest and highest; Bannockburn (a registered GI since 2022) and the Cromwell Basin (Bendigo, Pisa, Lowburn) are the warmest and give the most powerful wines; Alexandra is dry and continental; Wanaka is lakeside and cool. Felton Road (Bannockburn), Rippon (Wanaka), Burn Cottage, Prophet's Rock (Bendigo) and Two Paddocks are among the leaders.",
  "Schist-derived gravels and loess (windblown silt), very low rainfall (often under 400 mm), high sunshine and big temperature swings. Most vineyards are irrigated.",
  ["Felton Road", "Rippon", "Burn Cottage", "Prophet's Rock", "Two Paddocks", "Gibbston Valley", "Chard Farm", "Mount Difficulty", "Quartz Reef", "Valli", "Mount Edward", "Peregrine", "Amisfield"],
  "Rippon's vineyard on the shore of Lake Wanaka is one of the most photographed in the world; Nick Mills farms it biodynamically.",
  ["Bannockburn", "Gibbston (informal)", "Bendigo (informal)", "Cromwell Basin (informal)", "Pisa (informal)", "Lowburn (informal)", "Alexandra (informal)", "Wanaka (informal)"], ha=2099)
a("Bannockburn", 1028, "2022-02-01", "South Island", "Central Otago", ["red", "white"], "red", "Pinot Noir, Chardonnay, Riesling, Pinot Gris",
  "The most recent New Zealand wine GI, registered in February 2022, covering a small, warm sub-region of Central Otago on the southern bank of the Kawarau River near Cromwell. Gold miners sluiced its hills in the 1860s and 1870s, leaving the 'Cairnmuir' sluicings, an eerie landscape of cliffs and tailings. Stewart Elms planted Felton Road here in 1991 and 1992; with Mount Difficulty, Akarua and Carrick it made Bannockburn's ripe, structured, mineral Pinot Noir the most celebrated in the region.",
  "One of the warmest and driest corners of Central Otago, on schist-derived gravels, loess and clays over sandstone, at about 220 to 400 m.", ["Felton Road", "Mount Difficulty", "Akarua", "Carrick", "Terra Sancta", "Domaine Thomson"],
  "Felton Road's Block 3 and Block 5 Pinot Noirs come from parcels of the original Elms planting, on deeper loess and on a more exposed terrace respectively.", pt=(-45.0884, 169.16))

geo = geo_index("nz")
apps = []
for x in APPS:
    rec = {"country": "nz", "name": x["name"], "match": [x["name"]] + x["match"], "cat": "GI",
           "subcat": "Enduring GI" if x["no"] in (1001, 1002, 1003) else ("Sub-GI" if x["parent"] else "Region"),
           "region": x["island"], "subregion": x["parent"], "created": x["reg"]}
    if x["ha"]:
        rec["area_ha"] = x["ha"]
    rec.update({"styles": x["styles"], "dominant_style": x["dom"], "grapes": x["grapes"], "summary": x["summary"]})
    if x["terroir"]:
        rec["terroir"] = x["terroir"]
    if x["producers"]:
        rec["producers"] = x["producers"]
    if x["nerd"] and "shown as a point" not in x["nerd"]:
        rec["nerd_note"] = x["nerd"]
    if x["contains"]:
        rec["contains"] = x["contains"]
    rec["reg"] = {"iponz_no": x["no"]}
    if x["name"] not in geo:
        if x["pt"]:
            rec["lat"], rec["lon"] = x["pt"]
        else:
            print("NO POLYGON AND NO POINT", x["name"])
    apps.append(rec)
# fix Matakana nerd note (was a placeholder)
for r in apps:
    if r["name"] == "Matakana":
        r["nerd_note"] = "Matakana's vineyards share the district with olive groves and a Saturday farmers' market that draws Aucklanders north."
print("geo without content:", [n for n in geo if n not in [r["name"] for r in apps] and n not in ("Hawkes Bay", "Central Hawkes Bay", "Te Tai Tokerau")])

S = []


def s(*a, **k):
    S.append(site("nz", *a, **k))


s("Waitangi Treaty Grounds", "historic", -35.2663, 174.082, "James Busby, British Resident from 1833, planted vines beside his house at Waitangi, where the Treaty of Waitangi was signed on 6 February 1840. The French explorer Dumont d'Urville tasted Busby's wine here that year and described a light, sparkling white: the first recorded New Zealand wine. The house is now part of the Treaty Grounds.", appellation="Northland")
s("Kerikeri Mission Station", "historic", -35.2175, 173.9623, "Samuel Marsden, the Anglican missionary, planted about 100 vine cuttings at the Kerikeri mission in 1819, the first recorded in New Zealand. The mission's Kemp House (1822) is the country's oldest surviving building.", appellation="Northland")
s("Mission Estate", "estate", -39.5185, 176.8427, "French Marist missionaries planted vines in Hawke's Bay in 1851 and moved to this hillside at Taradale in 1880. Mission Estate is New Zealand's oldest winery still under the same ownership, the Society of Mary; its seminary building now houses a restaurant.", appellation="Hawke's Bay", producers=["Mission Estate"])
s("Church Road", "estate", -39.5248, 176.845, "Founded in 1897 by Bartholomew Steinmetz, a Luxembourger who had worked at the Mission, and later run by Tom McDonald, whose Cabernet in the 1940s and 1950s was one of New Zealand's first serious reds. Pernod Ricard's Church Road now makes Bordeaux blends and Chardonnay here.", appellation="Hawke's Bay", producers=["Church Road"])
s("Te Mata Estate", "estate", -39.6708, 176.9139, "Founded in 1896 by Bernard Chambers on the limestone hills of Havelock North, Te Mata is New Zealand's oldest winery in continuous production. John Buck and Michael Morris bought it in 1974; their Coleraine, a Cabernet-Merlot blend first made in 1982, is the country's most celebrated red, and Elston is a benchmark Chardonnay.", appellation="Hawke's Bay", cls="Coleraine (from 1982)", producers=["Te Mata Estate"])
s("Craggy Range", "estate", -39.6927, 176.923, "Terry Peabody, an Australian businessman, founded Craggy Range in 1997 with viticulturist Steve Smith MW, at the foot of Te Mata Peak. It farms vineyards in the Gimblett Gravels (Sophia, Le Sol Syrah) and at Te Muna Road in Martinborough.", appellation="Hawke's Bay", producers=["Craggy Range"])
s("Gimblett Gravels", "landscape", -39.6129, 176.7466, "About 800 ha of stony former riverbed west of Hastings, exposed when a great flood in 1867 shifted the Ngaruroro River. The gravels store heat and drain freely, ripening Syrah, Merlot and Cabernet. Its winegrowers trademarked the name in 2001 and only wines with 95 percent Gimblett Gravels fruit may use it.", appellation="Hawke's Bay", cls="Trademarked district (not a registered GI)", producers=["Trinity Hill", "Stonecroft", "Craggy Range", "Te Awa", "Mills Reef"])
s("Bridge Pa Triangle", "landscape", -39.6506, 176.7555, "A triangle of land south of the Gimblett Gravels around the village of Bridge Pa, on 'red metal' soils (iron-stained gravels) and silt loams. It ripens Merlot and Syrah with a softer, more fragrant profile than the gravels and has organised itself as a wine district.", appellation="Hawke's Bay", cls="Informal district", producers=["Sileni", "Ngatarawa", "Alpha Domus"], q="approx")
s("Stonecroft", "estate", -39.5962, 176.7455, "Alan Limmer, a soil scientist, planted Syrah at Stonecroft in 1984 and released New Zealand's first modern Syrah from the 1989 vintage, starting the variety's rise in Hawke's Bay.", appellation="Hawke's Bay", producers=["Stonecroft"])
s("Trinity Hill", "estate", -39.6146, 176.7338, "Founded in 1993 by John Hancock with London restaurateurs Robert and Robyn Wilson. Trinity Hill was one of the first to plant on Gimblett Road and helped create the Gimblett Gravels district; its Homage Syrah is a benchmark.", appellation="Hawke's Bay", producers=["Trinity Hill"])
s("Babich Wines", "estate", -36.8783, 174.6035, "Josip Babich, a Dalmatian immigrant, made his first wine in 1916 in Northland and moved to Henderson in 1919. The family still runs the business, one of the oldest of the West Auckland Croatian wineries.", appellation="Auckland", producers=["Babich"])
s("Kumeu River", "estate", -36.778, 174.5638, "The Brajkovich family bought this Kumeu property in 1944. Michael Brajkovich, New Zealand's first Master of Wine (1989), transformed it into one of the world's great Chardonnay producers; Maté's Vineyard, planted in 1990, is named after his father.", appellation="Kumeu", producers=["Kumeu River"])
s("Stonyridge", "estate", -36.7967, 175.0662, "Stephen White planted Stonyridge at Onetangi on Waiheke Island in 1981. Its Larose, a Bordeaux-style blend, became one of New Zealand's most sought-after reds and made the island a wine destination.", appellation="Waiheke Island", producers=["Stonyridge"])
s("Man O' War", "estate", -36.7857, 175.1543, "A large estate on the remote eastern end of Waiheke Island, on land named after Captain Cook's search for masts. Its vineyards spread across steep slopes and coves facing the Hauraki Gulf.", appellation="Waiheke Island", producers=["Man O' War"])
s("Millton Vineyards", "estate", -38.677, 177.8855, "James and Annie Millton founded their estate at Manutuke near Gisborne in 1984; it became New Zealand's first certified organic winery and later biodynamic. Clos de Ste Anne, on a steep clay-limestone hill, gives Chenin Blanc, Chardonnay and Syrah of real distinction.", appellation="Gisborne", producers=["Millton"], q="approx")
s("Ata Rangi", "estate", -41.2159, 175.468, "Clive Paton planted Ata Rangi on a stony former sheep paddock at Martinborough in 1980. Its Pinot Noir, built on the 'Abel' clone, is one of New Zealand's most admired reds; the name means 'new beginning' or 'dawn sky' in Māori.", appellation="Martinborough", producers=["Ata Rangi"])
s("Dry River", "estate", -41.2866, 175.418, "Neil and Dawn McCallum planted Dry River in 1979 after Derek Milne's report on Martinborough. Their Pinot Noir, Gewürztraminer and Riesling, made to age, became some of New Zealand's most collected wines.", appellation="Martinborough", producers=["Dry River"], q="approx")
s("Escarpment", "estate", -41.2625, 175.4793, "Larry McKenna, often called the 'Prince of Pinot' for his work at Martinborough Vineyard, founded Escarpment on Te Muna Road in 1999, making single-vineyard Pinot Noirs from old Martinborough sites.", appellation="Martinborough", producers=["Escarpment"], q="approx")
s("Neudorf Vineyards", "estate", -41.2468, 172.9924, "Tim and Judy Finn planted Neudorf in the Moutere Hills of Nelson in 1978. Its Moutere Chardonnay, from clay-gravel soils, is one of New Zealand's finest, and the estate now belongs to a group led by Tim and Judy's daughter and partners.", appellation="Nelson", producers=["Neudorf"])
s("Brancott Estate", "estate", -41.5401, 173.841, "Montana Wines planted the first commercial vineyard in Marlborough here in 1973. The first Marlborough Sauvignon Blanc followed in 1979. Now owned by Pernod Ricard and renamed Brancott Estate, its hilltop heritage centre overlooks the vineyards where the style was born.", appellation="Marlborough", cls="First Marlborough vineyard (1973)", producers=["Brancott Estate"])
s("Cloudy Bay", "estate", -41.4917, 173.8795, "David Hohnen of Cape Mentelle founded Cloudy Bay in 1985 with winemaker Kevin Judd, naming it after the bay Captain Cook named in 1770. Its Sauvignon Blanc and misty-mountain label made Marlborough famous worldwide; Te Koko, a barrel-fermented Sauvignon, showed another side. LVMH owns it.", appellation="Marlborough", producers=["Cloudy Bay"])
s("Wairau Valley", "landscape", -41.472, 173.893, "The broad, flat heart of Marlborough along the Wairau River. Its northern side around Rapaura has stony, free-draining alluvial soils (called 'stoney' by locals) that give the classic tropical, pungent Sauvignon Blanc.", appellation="Marlborough", cls="Informal subregion", q="approx")
s("Southern Valleys", "landscape", -41.5878, 173.8501, "A series of side valleys south of the Wairau (Brancott, Omaka, Fairhall, Ben Morven, Waihopai), with clay-rich soils that give more textured Sauvignon Blanc and Marlborough's best Pinot Noir and Chardonnay.", appellation="Marlborough", cls="Informal subregion", producers=["Dog Point", "Clos Henri", "Seresin", "Churton"], q="approx")
s("Awatere Valley", "landscape", -41.6712, 174.0734, "A higher, windier and cooler valley south-east of the Wairau around Seddon, planted on a large scale only from the late 1990s. Its Sauvignon Blanc is leaner and more herbal, with tomato-leaf and saline notes.", appellation="Marlborough", cls="Informal subregion", producers=["Yealands", "Vavasour", "Tohu"], q="approx")
s("Yealands Estate", "estate", -41.6483, 174.1319, "Peter Yealands planted his estate near the coast at Seaview in the Awatere Valley from 2003, terracing hills with bulldozers. It markets itself on sustainability, including grazing miniature sheep in the vines.", appellation="Marlborough", producers=["Yealands"])
s("Pyramid Valley", "estate", -42.9646, 172.6108, "Mike and Claudia Weersing planted Pyramid Valley in 2000 on limestone near Waikari after a long search for the right soil. Their biodynamic, block-named Pinot Noirs and Chardonnays were acclaimed; Brian Sheth and Steve Smith bought the estate in 2017.", appellation="North Canterbury", producers=["Pyramid Valley"])
s("Bell Hill", "estate", -43.0075, 172.7013, "Marcel Giesen and Sherwyn Veldhuizen planted Bell Hill on an old limestone quarry at Weka Pass in 1997, densely, Burgundy-style. Its tiny production of Pinot Noir and Chardonnay is among New Zealand's most collected.", appellation="North Canterbury", producers=["Bell Hill"], q="approx")
s("Pegasus Bay", "estate", -43.0919, 172.7689, "The Donaldson family (neurologist Ivan Donaldson and his wife Christine) planted Pegasus Bay in the Waipara Valley in 1985. Its Rieslings, from dry to the botrytised Aria, and its Pinot Noir made Waipara's reputation.", appellation="Waipara Valley", producers=["Pegasus Bay"])
s("Rippon", "estate", -44.6949, 169.1047, "Rolfe and Lois Mills planted Rippon on the shore of Lake Wanaka in 1975, one of the first modern vineyards in Central Otago. Their son Nick Mills, who trained in Burgundy, farms it biodynamically; the view across the lake to the mountains is one of the most photographed in wine.", appellation="Central Otago", producers=["Rippon"])
s("Felton Road", "estate", -45.0892, 169.1611, "Stewart Elms planted Felton Road at Bannockburn in 1991 and 1992; Englishman Nigel Greening bought it in 2000, and Blair Walter has made the wines since the start. Its biodynamic, block-designated Pinot Noir (Block 3, Block 5, Calvert, Cornish Point) is the benchmark for Central Otago.", appellation="Bannockburn", producers=["Felton Road"])
s("Gibbston Valley Winery", "estate", -45.0123, 168.9152, "Alan Brady, a journalist from Northern Ireland, planted vines in the Kawarau Gorge in 1981 and released Gibbston Valley's first commercial wine in 1987. Its schist cave, dug into the hill in 1995, is a tourist landmark.", appellation="Central Otago", producers=["Gibbston Valley"])
s("Chard Farm", "estate", -45.0118, 168.8807, "Rob and Greg Hay planted Chard Farm on a ledge above the Kawarau Gorge in 1987, reached by a cliff-edge road; it is one of the pioneers of Central Otago Pinot Noir.", appellation="Central Otago", producers=["Chard Farm"])
s("Bendigo", "landscape", -44.926, 169.3437, "A former gold-mining settlement on the eastern side of Lake Dunstan, now one of Central Otago's warmest districts, with north-facing schist terraces planted since the late 1990s. Prophet's Rock, Quartz Reef and Mount Edward have vineyards here.", appellation="Central Otago", cls="Informal subregion", producers=["Prophet's Rock", "Quartz Reef"], q="approx")
s("Gibbston", "landscape", -45.0233, 168.9552, "The coolest and highest of Central Otago's sub-regions, along the Kawarau Gorge near Queenstown at up to about 420 m, where Pinot Noir is lighter and more perfumed.", appellation="Central Otago", cls="Informal subregion", producers=["Gibbston Valley", "Peregrine", "Valli"], q="approx")
s("Cromwell Basin (Lowburn and Pisa)", "landscape", -45.0041, 169.2127, "The broad basin around Lake Dunstan and Cromwell, including Lowburn and the Pisa terraces on the west bank, holds the largest share of Central Otago's vines. It is warm and dry, and gives ripe, dark-fruited Pinot Noir.", appellation="Central Otago", cls="Informal subregion", producers=["Burn Cottage", "Two Paddocks"], q="approx")
s("Alexandra", "landscape", -45.2551, 169.3922, "The southernmost Central Otago sub-region, around the town of Alexandra in the Clutha Valley, among the driest places in New Zealand, with large daily temperature swings. Grasshopper Rock, Two Paddocks and Black Ridge make wine here.", appellation="Central Otago", cls="Informal subregion", q="approx")
s("Burn Cottage", "estate", -45.0164, 169.1866, "A biodynamic Pinot Noir estate at Lowburn founded in 2002 by the American Sauvage family, with Ted Lemon of Littorai as an adviser.", appellation="Central Otago", producers=["Burn Cottage"], q="approx")
s("Te Kauwhata Viticultural Research Station", "historic", -37.4035, 175.1468, "The government research station where Romeo Bragato worked as the first government viticulturist from 1902, testing grape varieties and phylloxera-resistant rootstocks. It shaped New Zealand viticulture for most of the twentieth century.", appellation="North Island", q="approx")

